# Portable console specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies mm.stdio and its Pico SDK USB CDC provider for release
v1.2.0. It describes implemented behavior. docs/modules-platforms.mdy owns the
platform-interface and platform-provider mechanism; docs/modules-platform-pico.mdy
owns the Pico platform instance; docs/modules-c++20.mdy owns the adapter and
linkage-shim allowances.

# Role

mm.stdio is one platform-selected console. It moves bytes to and from wherever
the selected platform assigns that role. It is not formatting, a line
discipline, or a hardware UART.

mm.mcu:uart answers “write to this UART peripheral.” mm.stdio answers “write to
this platform's console.” USB CDC, a debugger's semihosting channel, and a UART
may each carry a console, but that does not make them the same peripheral.

# Interface

Opening fence
enum class Status {
    Ok,
    BadArgument,
    Unsupported,
    NotInitialized,
    Busy,
    Timeout,
    TransportError
};

class Console {
    virtual Status initialize();
    virtual Status write(std::span<const std::byte> data,
                         std::size_t& written);
    virtual Status read(std::span<std::byte> data, std::size_t& count);
    virtual Status flush();
    virtual Status connected(bool& value);
};

void set_console(Console& console);
Console& selected_console();
Closing fence

Output parameters change only when the operation returns Ok. write may report
fewer bytes than the input span, including zero; the caller resumes at that
offset and must bound its retries. read returns Ok with count zero when no byte
is waiting. connected is a query, never a wait. A caller that waits polls it and
owns the stopping condition.

The linkable fallback answers Unsupported. It lets the module and host tests
exist without a selected target platform and never guesses that a listener is
present.

The interface moves bytes. It provides no printf, iostream, locale, number
format, newline translation, echo, editing, baud, parity, or flow-control
policy.

# Pico USB CDC provider

platform.pico.stdio implements Console over Pico SDK's pico_stdio_usb driver.
Both pico-arm and pico-riscv bind it at SDK level:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.stdio platform.pico.stdio
Closing fence

Every SDK-backed board therefore receives the default. The ordinary provider
precedence rule still applies: a board binding mm.stdio replaces the SDK
default for that board. The generic board-over-SDK fixture in the mm.build
provider suite proves this rule independently of USB hardware.

The bridge enables USB and keeps semihosting enabled. Existing lib.pico output
continues through Pico's aggregate stdio path; mm.stdio addresses USB directly,
so its connected and byte-count answers are about CDC rather than an ambiguous
union of enabled outputs.

The bridge defines PICO_STDIO_USB_CONNECTION_WITHOUT_DTR=1. connected therefore
reports USB enumeration through tud_ready(), not the host's DTR line. A terminal
that does not assert DTR cannot leave a bounded caller waiting forever for a
signal unrelated to enumeration. Enumeration does not prove that the host is
draining output.

Pico SDK performs USB work from its default timer and low-priority IRQ. A caller
does not call tud_task and mm.stdio exposes no pump operation.

# Delivery limits

The SDK defaults its initial connection wait to zero, so output offered before
enumeration is discarded. The Pico provider makes that visible as Ok with
written equal to zero. A program that needs its first message observed polls
connected before writing and bounds that poll.

Pico SDK also gives up when a connected host stops draining output for its
configured stdout timeout. Its public USB output callback returns void, and
stdio_put_string always returns the requested length. The provider can therefore
report disconnected zero-versus-connected acceptance, but cannot observe a
mid-call timeout after only part of a connected transfer physically reached the
host. written in that case is the size accepted by the SDK call, not a receipt
from the host. Portable callers must already handle short writes and must not
treat a full count as end-to-end delivery acknowledgement.

The SDK's connect-wait timeout is zero, so the conditional wait block containing
its post-connect delay is compiled out. The provider adds no delay of its own. A
terminal may therefore lose bytes sent immediately after enumeration; hardware
protocols needing acknowledgement must define one above this byte transport.

# Private boundary

The provider includes its colocated stdio-cxx.h linkage shim plus the standard
<cstddef> and <span> headers, and imports mm.stdio. It includes no Pico SDK
header. The shim wraps stdio-c.h in C language linkage. The bridge's existing
adapter.c implements the five private mm_pico_stdio_* functions and remains the
only project-authored translation unit that includes Pico SDK headers.

The private ABI validates null pointers and lengths before converting size_t to
the SDK's int lengths. Integer status values cross only that C boundary; the
provider maps every value back to mm::stdio::Status.

# Application and qualification

apps/stdio-smoke imports only mm.stdio. It treats an unserved host fallback as a
valid no-console host run. With a provider it polls connected with a fixed
iteration bound, reports failure if that bound expires, writes without resending
an accepted prefix, and flushes. The bound guarantees termination but is not a
wall-clock duration; adding mm.mcu only to measure this smoke test would defeat
the proof that an application naming only mm.stdio receives its provider.

scripts/build-stdio-smoke-pico-sdk.sh builds one selected Pico SDK board,
checks its ELF and UF2, verifies exactly one platform.pico.stdio initializer,
and builds target-smoke-any to prove an unrelated executable contains none. It
does not claim that a person saw bytes. Hardware qualification flashes the UF2,
opens its CDC terminal, resets the board, and observes:

Opening fence
modules.cpp mm.stdio over USB CDC
Closing fence

# Conformance

- mm.stdio builds and its host stand-in passes without Pico SDK or hardware.
- A disconnected stand-in reports Ok with a zero written count; a bounded
  connected stand-in reports accepted bytes without hanging.
- Both Pico SDK manifests bind platform.pico.stdio, while a board binding the
  same interface overrides that default through the shared precedence rule.
- A Pico image reaching mm.stdio contains exactly one provider initializer; an
  image not reaching it contains none.
- USB connection testing ignores DTR, USB background service needs no caller
  task loop, and semihosting remains enabled for existing wrapper applications.
- Host build, all unit suites, check, model, and document pass with no board.

# Current boundaries

- No automated test asserts that bytes appeared at a host terminal.
- A connected full write count is not an end-to-end delivery acknowledgement,
  because Pico SDK exposes no byte count from its timeout-capable USB callback.
- platform.linux.stdio provides a hosted Linux console through descriptors zero
  and one. It installs SIG_IGN for SIGPIPE, tracks output and input loss
  independently, and reports output connectivity without consulting a device
  map. Project-linked bare-metal boards still have no console provider.
- USB classes other than CDC, USB host mode, and reset-to-BOOTSEL are outside
  mm.stdio.
